+Each run of a registered test lasts 8700\,s and is judged over $[t_b+4500,\,8700]$\,s; in the rule, $\lmiss$ is the cell's $\lref$. A BURST run is TRAPPED if $\lambda>\lmiss$ and (a) its uncached prefill token rate is within $\pm15\%$ of the cell's E0a all-miss rate (11,243 tokens/s at 300k) and (b) the slope of its backlog exceeds $0.5(\lambda-\lmiss)$, the slope being the least-squares slope of 10\,s bin means of the sessions in the system (arrived and not ended, including any held at the gate) over the window. A run is RECOVERED if over the last 300\,s its hit fraction exceeds 0.8 and its outstanding backlog (requests in flight plus sessions held at the gate) is below 20; NOBURST is good if RECOVERED. A run that is neither is DRAINING if its slope is negative and INDETERMINATE otherwise; the labels take precedence in the order trapped, recovered, draining, indeterminate. A rate PASSES if, in at least two of three seeds, BURST is trapped, NOBURST good and CAPPED recovered in that same seed. Criterion (a) uses tokens because a trapped engine with a growing backlog completes sessions at a rate other than $\lmiss$ (faster for our workload, slower with a 20-step budget, \S\ref{sec:mirror}) while it prefills at the all-miss token rate (Theorem~\ref{thm:premium}, \S\ref{sec:validation}); the paragraphs below give the evidence.
 
 The registration was filled by formula from committed copies of the Stage~C curve, the E0a calibration and the E0b bracket: the window $(1,\,2.0)\,\lref$, its upper edge E0b's largest all-stable multiple; the rates one third and two thirds of the way across it, rounded to 0.05 (1.35 and 1.65 $\lref$), each of which must lie strictly between 1.1 times the window's lower edge and 0.9 times its upper edge, here $(1.1,\,1.8)\,\lref$, or the fill returns INCOMPLETE; and the cap, the smallest $n$ with $\mu(n)\ge0.95\mu^*$, which gives $\ncap=20$. We call a test pre-registered when its registration file was committed and pushed to GitHub before its runs, with GitHub's push log of our development repository as the record (not an entry in a third-party registry; pre-registered gates have been used in ML-systems measurement before~\citep{suram2026cacheable}); that repository is private, so readers see the log only as our export of it in \fpath{provenance/} (Appendix~\ref{app:prov}), which cannot be checked against GitHub independently. We call a test registered when a FILLED registration file (Table~\ref{tab:ledger}) binds its runs and was committed before its jobs were submitted; neither label says whether a prediction for the test was blind (\S\ref{sec:frozen}). The E2 and native tests are pre-registered, and so is every later registered test but one, although the text mostly calls these simply registered: the rescue tests R1 and R4 (\S\ref{sec:rescue}), the basin tests BASIN-1 and BASIN-2 (\S\ref{sec:basin}) and the TOOLSLOW and SHED tests, whose registrations reached GitHub 22 to 131 minutes before their jobs were submitted, and the 600k H200 and Llama-3.1-8B paired tests, whose registrations reached it 63 and 47\,s before theirs (\S\ref{sec:h200llama}). The exception is vLLM: its registration reached GitHub after its jobs were submitted, when two of them had started, so that test is registered, not pre-registered. On the H200 with the 300k pool the rule could not place 